Two points:

1) The browser client must still go through some endpoint to avoid CORS/authentication issues when calling OpenRouter. So a small proxy pass-through is expected (we just don’t require a custom user-supplied proxy).

2) The previous single-file demo ran through a tiny local proxy injected in the HTML. Now that we split files, we need a tiny runtime proxy module that forwards to openrouter (handles auth, forwards headers/body, and returns the stream). Your HTML should load that module and point the fetch at it, not at an optional custom URL.

Approach:
- Provide a runtime proxy module: tiny code that reads OPENROUTER_API_KEY from the environment and forwards to https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions. If you deploy this to Workers, you don’t need the earlier custom proxy input at all.
- Have the UI call a fixed runtime proxy (e.g., https://ai.nspired.cc/chat or whatever you deploy) instead of asking for a custom URL. That keeps CORS/auth handled and removes the optional “custom proxy” input. If you still want a custom override, keep the input but default to the runtime proxy.

